PLACES=[
N('round-lake-auditorium','Round Lake camp-meeting village and Auditorium','Round Lake','sar','town','standing',42.93677,-73.79384,'exact','view','walk','','1868–',
  'Round Lake began in 1867–68 as a summer camp meeting of Methodists from Troy, owned and run by the Round Lake Association, with crowds the Auditorium\'s history page puts at up to 20,000. Tents gave way to cottages, and by the 1880s it had become a Chautauqua-style resort with hotels, lectures and George West\'s museum (built 1887, taken down in 1965). The association faded and the village was incorporated in 1969; the whole village is a historic district on the Register (1975).',
  'The village is the place: close-set Gothic, Stick and Second Empire cottages on narrow lanes, the reason for the 1975 listing. In the middle stands the Auditorium at 2 Wesley Avenue, grown from a platform in the trees to a tent, an open pavilion and in 1912 the glass-walled hall seen today, seating 300. Inside is the Davis-Ferris tracker organ, built in New York City in 1847 and moved here in 1888 (on the Register by itself, 100000864). The sources differ on it: Wikipedia gives 1,980 pipes, the Auditorium\'s page 1,900, and that page calls it a National Historic Landmark designated in 2016 on its history page and 2017 on its home page. Concerts and events run from late May to early October.',
  'A Methodist tent ground that turned into a village, with an 1847 organ still played in its auditorium.',
  'The village is public streets; the cottages are private homes, seen from the road. The Auditorium belongs to the village and opens for its summer events (late May to early October, per its page); at other times look from Wesley Avenue. Where to park in the village not checked. Interior seen only at events.',
  [W('Round Lake (village), New York'),L('Round Lake Auditorium: history','https://www.roundlakeauditorium.org/history'),L('Round Lake Auditorium','https://www.roundlakeauditorium.org/')],conf='mixed',brick='?'),
N('ruhle-road-lenticular-bridge','Ruhle Road lenticular truss bridge','Malta','sar','landmark','standing',42.95832,-73.81670,'exact','public','walk','','1888; here since 2001',
  'An iron lenticular ("lens") truss built in 1888 by the Berlin Iron Bridge Company of East Berlin, Connecticut, for a road over Black Creek in Salem, Washington County. In 2001 it was taken apart and set up again over Ballston Creek in Malta, about 30 miles away, where a stone arch bridge of about 1870 (on the Register in 1990) had washed out in a 1993 freshet. It was listed on the Register in 2004.',
  'A single 57-foot span, 14 feet wide, with the curved top and bottom chords that give the type its name, over a deep cut of Ballston Creek. The new abutments are faced with stone saved from the fallen 1870 arch bridge. Plaques on the top chords name the 1888 builders and the 2001 move. It carries a footpath (mapped as "Ruhle Pass") between the ends of Ruhle Road North and Ruhle Road South; no bicycles on the mapped path.',
  'A bridge that walked 30 miles: an 1888 lenticular truss standing on the stones of the arch it replaced.',
  'A public footpath; the Register form calls the setting a public park, but which body owns it was not found. Ruhle Road North and South are residential and no parking lot is mapped near the bridge; where to park not checked. Hours not checked.',
  [W('Ruhle Road Lenticular Metal Truss Bridge'),L('National Register nomination, 2004 (historicbridges.org copy)','https://historicbridges.org/newyork/ruhlereplacement/nomination.pdf')],brick='no'),
N('dunning-street-cemetery-malta','Dunning Street Cemetery','Malta','sar','burial','standing',42.97139,-73.79556,'site','public','drive','cemeteries','about 1775–',
  'A rural burying ground begun about 1775 on Dunning Street in Malta and run by the Dunning Street Cemetery Association since 1908. Many early Malta settlers lie here, among them veterans of the Revolutionary War. It was listed on the Register in 2015.',
  'Nineteenth-century gravestones that the Register listing names as good local funerary art, and the graves of Revolutionary War veterans. It is still an active burial ground.',
  '',
  'Owned by the Dunning Street Cemetery Association; an active cemetery, so visit quietly. Hours and parking not checked.',
  [W('Dunning Street Cemetery')],brick='?'),
N('brookside-museum-ballston-spa','Brookside: the Aldridge inn (Brookside Museum)','Ballston Spa','sar','hotel','standing',43.00218,-73.85517,'exact','open','drive','hotels','1792–',
  'Benajah Douglas, grandfather of Stephen A. Douglas, built the main block about 1792–93 as a guest house beside the spring that drew visitors to Ballston Spa; Josiah Aldridge, owner from 1798, added the rear wing, a ballroom wing (later moved away) and the colonnade. James Fenimore Cooper is said to have written part of The Last of the Mohicans while staying here. The spring failed in 1835 and the house stopped taking guests in 1846; it was later a boys\' school, a boarding house, a sanatorium and apartments, until the Saratoga County Historical Society bought it in 1970.',
  'A two-and-a-half-storey clapboard house on a fieldstone foundation with a full-height porch on four square pillars, a cedar-shingle roof and brick end chimneys, on the triangle where Front Street splits into Charlton and Fairground avenues. Inside, little original woodwork survives; the staircase dates from 1860 and the framing is hand-hewn. It is the Saratoga County History Center\'s museum, open to visitors.',
  'One of Ballston Spa\'s first spa inns, now the county\'s history museum.',
  'Owned by the Saratoga County History Center. Hours from its visit page. Wikipedia notes a small dirt parking area on the south side.',
  [W('Brookside Museum'),L('Saratoga County History Center: visit','https://brooksidemuseum.org/visit/')],brick='some',hours='Wednesday to Sunday, noon to 4 p.m.'),
N('union-mill-ballston-spa','Union Mill and the West bag factory','Ballston Spa','sar','industry','standing',43.00833,-73.85028,'site','view','drive','','1844–',
  'Hezekiah Middlebrook dammed the Kayaderosseras Creek here about 1830; a cotton mill followed in 1844 and a woolen mill in 1850. After the cotton mill burned in 1878, George West, who made his fortune with a flat-bottomed paper bag, bought the site and built the present Union Paper Mill, turned the woolen mill into a bag factory and added a showpiece office. From 1918 it was Bischoff\'s chocolate factory, then a warehouse; it was listed on the Register in 1982 and is now a retail complex.',
  'Three brick buildings on Milton Avenue (NY 50) at Prospect Street: the four-storey L-shaped mill with two stair towers, corbelled brickwork and a clock in one dormer (its tower holds a 1,500 lb bell cast by Jones & Co. of Troy), the long bag factory with its metal roof monitors, and the towered office building. The Register form noted oak and cherry wainscoting and walk-in vaults inside and no machinery left. Behind, a few stone piers of the Blue Mill Dam remain in the creek. Present tenants and whether the interiors can be seen not checked.',
  'The paper-bag king\'s mill, later a chocolate factory.',
  'Privately owned commercial buildings; see them from Milton Avenue and Prospect Street. Opening of the shops and parking not checked.',
  [W('Union Mill Complex')],brick='yes'),
N('saratoga-gasholder-house','Saratoga gasholder house and gas works','Saratoga Springs','sar','industry','standing',43.0905,-73.7745,'site','view','drive','','1873–',
  'The Saratoga Gas Light Company built a new gas works here in 1873–74, near the railroad and Village Brook and on a deep clay bed that sealed its gasholder pits. It became the Saratoga Gas, Electric Light and Power Company in 1887, the resort\'s first power station. Gas making stopped in 1928; utilities kept using the site until Niagara Mohawk gave it up in 1992, and it was listed on the Register in 2001.',
  'A round brick gasholder house, 70 feet across with a conical slate roof and six brick buttresses (Wikipedia calls it one of only 13 of its kind left in the Northeast), near the junction of East Avenue with US 9 and NY 50, and 200 feet south of it a two-storey brick substation with panelled walls and an oculus in each gable. The 1998 HAER photograph and the Register text describe the whole site fenced, gravelled and screened by trees along the highway. Whether it is still fenced and empty in 2026 not checked.',
  'One of the last conical-roofed gasholder houses in the Northeast, in plain sight of the Northway traffic into Saratoga.',
  'Private, fenced utility land (as described for the Register). See it from East Avenue or Excelsior Avenue; do not go in. Present owner not checked.',
  [W('Saratoga Gas, Electric Light and Power Company Complex')],conf='mixed',brick='yes'),
N('drinkhall-saratoga-springs','The Drink Hall (trolley station, now the visitor center)','Saratoga Springs','sar','rail','standing',43.07828,-73.78670,'exact','open','walk','stations','1915–',
  'Built in 1915 as a trolley station for the Hudson Valley Railroad, to a Beaux Arts design by Ludlow and Peabody, and later known as the Drink Hall. It was listed on the Register in 1974 and is now the Saratoga Springs Heritage Area Visitor Center.',
  'A stuccoed central block of three bays with lower wings either side, built of hollow red clay tile under a slate hip roof, with a frieze of arrowhead motifs. The open porch at the back was the trolley platform. Inside, the central hall keeps its high barrel-vaulted ceiling, open to visitors during visitor-center hours.',
  '',
  "Visitor center at 297 Broadway, run by Discover Saratoga. Hours from its services page. Wikipedia's coordinate is about 1 km off; the pin is the OpenStreetMap point for 297 Broadway.",
  [W('Saratoga Springs Visitor Center'),L('Discover Saratoga: visitor center services and hours','https://www.discoversaratoga.org/saratoga-springs-visitor-center/services/')],brick='no',hours='January to March: Tuesday to Saturday 10 a.m. to 4 p.m. April to December: Tuesday to Saturday 9 a.m. to 5 p.m.'),
N('yaddo-gardens','Yaddo and its gardens','Saratoga Springs','sar','inst','standing',43.06912,-73.75802,'site','open','walk','','1881–',
  'The financier Spencer Trask and the writer Katrina Trask bought the estate in 1881; the first mansion burned in 1891 and they built the present one. After their four children died young, Trask set the estate up as a retreat for artists, with help from George Foster Peabody, and the first artists arrived in 1926. Yaddo is still an artists\' colony and was made a National Historic Landmark in 2013.',
  'The mansion and studios are private and closed to visitors. The gardens are open: a formal rose garden (in bloom from mid-June through September) with a central fountain, terraces, a balcony and a rose-covered pergola, and a rock garden with its own fountain among trees. The New York by Rail page describes docent tours from the main fountain on summer weekends in 2019; current tours not checked.',
  'The public garden of the retreat whose guests have won 82 Pulitzer Prizes between them.',
  'The gardens are open to the public year-round from dawn to dusk (New York by Rail, a state tourism partner; Yaddo\'s own site refused the request, so not confirmed with the owner). The rest of the estate is a working artists\' residence: keep to the gardens. Parking not checked.',
  [W('Yaddo'),L('New York by Rail: Yaddo Gardens','https://www.newyorkbyrail.com/local-guide/yaddo-gardens/')],conf='mixed',brick='?'),
N('gideon-putnam-burying-ground','Gideon Putnam Burying Ground','Saratoga Springs','sar','burial','standing',43.07889,-73.79097,'site','view','walk','cemeteries','1812–1871',
  'Gideon Putnam, who laid out Saratoga Springs and built its first resort hotel, set aside this ground for a public cemetery in his 1810 plan and became its first burial in 1812 after a fall from scaffolding at Congress Hall. The family let others be buried here and gave it to the village in 1835; burials stopped in 1871. Neglected for about a century, it was restored in the 1980s and listed on the Register in 2003.',
  'About 160 marked graves of 1812 to 1871, most in marble or granite, some carved with urns and willows, scattered without rows over 1.1 acres. On a rise is the Putnam family plot inside a fieldstone wall with a wrought-iron gate; obelisks mark Gideon Putnam and his wife. It is his only trace left in the city he founded.',
  '',
  'City cemetery on the east side of South Franklin Street just south of Grand Avenue, behind a chain-link fence along the street. Whether a gate is open to walk in not checked; it can be seen from South Franklin Street.',
  [W('Gideon Putnam Burying Ground')],brick='no'),
N('saratoga-spa-bathhouses','Saratoga Spa State Park: the bathhouses and Hall of Springs','Saratoga Springs','sar','inst','standing',43.06462,-73.79037,'area','public','walk','','1930s–',
  'Pumping for bottled water and carbonation nearly drained Saratoga\'s springs by 1908, when the state stepped in to protect them. In the 1930s Reconstruction Finance Corporation money built the park\'s classical bath houses, research buildings and a drink hall; after the Second World War veterans and Holocaust survivors took the waters here. Wikipedia notes that in 2007 the state admitted the "pure" mineral water in the public baths had been mixed with tap water.',
  'The pin is the Lincoln Bathhouse at 65 South Broadway, now the park office and welcome center. Further in are the Roosevelt Baths (still a mineral bath and spa), the Hall of Springs (a banquet hall) and the Gideon Putnam hotel, with Geyser Island Spouter and the Orenda Spring along Geyser Creek. Inside the Lincoln Bathhouse the park lists a Saratoga Museum of Hydrotherapy; what original fittings survive inside the buildings not checked.',
  'A whole New Deal spa, built to save the springs from being pumped dry.',
  'State park (NYS Parks). The welcome center in the Lincoln Bathhouse is the place to ask; the park lists a vehicle use fee. Park hours are not printed on the details page, which says they change and to call ahead. Parking and restrooms are available.',
  [W('Saratoga Spa State Park'),SPA],brick='?'),
N('canfield-casino-congress-park','Canfield Casino and Congress Park','Saratoga Springs','sar','inst','standing',43.07856,-73.78387,'exact','public','walk','','1870–',
  'Congress Spring, named in 1792, drew Gideon Putnam to build Congress Hall beside it; the park grew on the ground of that hotel and of the Congress Spring bottling plant. The casino\'s main building went up in 1870, with additions in 1871 and 1902–03, and it was a famous gambling house until reformers ended gambling in 1907. The park and casino were made a National Historic Landmark district in 1987.',
  'Greek-style pavilions over the springs (a Doric one on the site of the original Congress Spring, a domed one over the Columbian Spring of Gideon Putnam, restored in 1983), Italian gardens, a statue by Daniel Chester French and landscape work credited to Frederick Law Olmsted. Near the Spring Street entrance is a carousel whose 28 horses were carved in 1904 by Marcus Charles Illions. The casino, a four-storey red-brick Italianate block (photograph on Wikipedia), holds the Saratoga Springs History Museum; which original rooms and fittings survive not checked.',
  '',
  'Congress Park is a City of Saratoga Springs park bounded by Broadway, Spring Street and Circular Street. The museum in the casino is open Wednesday to Sunday, 10 am to 4 pm (its home page, read November 2026); admission charged. Parking not checked.',
  [W('Canfield Casino and Congress Park'),L('Saratoga Springs History Museum','https://www.saratogahistory.org/')],brick='yes',was='lm-canfield-casino-and-congress-park'),
N('grant-cottage-mount-mcgregor','Grant Cottage','Mount McGregor','sar','landmark','standing',43.20131,-73.74532,'exact','open','drive','','1885',
  'Ulysses S. Grant spent the last six weeks of his life at this cottage of the banker Joseph W. Drexel on Mount McGregor and died of throat cancer here on July 23, 1885, three days after finishing the memoirs that saved his widow from poverty. Kept as a shrine by caretakers and the Mount McGregor Memorial Association, it became a state historic site in 1957 and a National Historic Landmark in 2021.',
  'The cottage is kept as it was on the day Grant died: tours of the ground floor show the original furnishings and his own things, the bed he died in and floral arrangements from his funeral. The grounds have an overlook where Grant liked to sit (now with a memorial to him), walking trails, panels and a visitor center.',
  'The room where Grant raced death to finish his memoirs.',
  'State of New York; run by the Friends of the U.S. Grant Cottage. Seasonal: its page says the site is closed for the season (read November 2026). When open, the grounds, overlook, trails and visitor center are free and the house is seen by guided tour. Parking lot beside the site.',
  [W('Grant Cottage State Historic Site'),L('Friends of the U.S. Grant Cottage','https://www.grantcottage.org/')],th=[43.20044,-73.74499],thn='Grant Cottage lot, Mt McGregor Road',brick='?',was='lm-grant-cottage'),
N('saratoga-battlefield','Saratoga Battlefield (Saratoga National Historical Park)','Stillwater','sar','fort','traces',42.98572,-73.64092,'area','public','drive','forts','1777',
  'In September and October 1777 American forces fought and stopped General Burgoyne\'s army on these farm fields above the Hudson, and forced it to surrender at Saratoga (Schuylerville) on October 17: the first great American victory of the war, which brought France in as an ally. The battlefield became a state preserve in 1927 and part of the National Park System in 1938.',
  'A tour road with numbered stops through the fields of the Freeman, Neilson, Chatfield and Barber farms. The pin is the Neilson Farm stop, where John Neilson\'s restored house (used as quarters by Generals Arnold and Poor) stands and posts outline the American lines. Posts also outline the Balcarres and Breymann redoubts, and near the Breymann Redoubt stands the "Boot" monument to Benedict Arnold, which does not name him (stop descriptions from CNY Hiking and the NPS tour pages). The Wilkinson Trail is a marked 4¼-mile loop.',
  'The fields where the Revolution turned, with the monument to the general it does not name.',
  'National Park Service; the park is fee-free. Visitor center at 648 Route 32, Stillwater. Grounds open daily sunrise to sunset; the tour road is open to cars 9 am to 4 pm and closes to cars on November 30, but stays open to walkers and cyclists sunrise to sunset; the Neilson House is closed for the 2026 season (NPS hours page, updated November 1, 2026). Trails are foot only. Parking at each tour stop.',
  [W('Saratoga National Historical Park'),NPSH,NPSD,L('NPS: Tour Stop 7, Breymann Redoubt','https://www.nps.gov/places/tour-stop-7-the-decisive-moment.htm'),L('CNY Hiking: Saratoga Battlefield auto tour road','https://cnyhiking.com/SaratogaBattlefieldAutoTourRoad.htm')],th=[42.98629,-73.64156],thn='Neilson Farm stop, battlefield tour road',brick='?'),
N('schuyler-house-schuylerville','General Schuyler House','Schuylerville','sar','landmark','standing',43.09576,-73.58177,'exact','public','drive','','1777',
  'The British burned General Philip Schuyler\'s country house and outbuildings here during their retreat in October 1777. Schuyler had the present house built in November 1777, in 29 days according to Wikipedia, and ran his farms and mills from it; Washington, Jefferson, Madison, Lafayette and Franklin Roosevelt all called here. It is now part of Saratoga National Historical Park, restored by the Park Service.',
  'A two-storey clapboard house (photograph on Wikipedia), the one Schuyler called his "commodious box", rebuilt with glass, nails, locks and hinges salvaged from the burned one according to Wikipedia, which also says it has no electricity or running water. The grounds are open all year; the house itself opens seasonally (Wikipedia says late May to Labor Day).',
  '',
  'National Park Service. Grounds open sunrise to sunset; the house is closed for the 2026 season (NPS hours page, November 2026). On US 4 about a mile south of the Burgoyne Road turn; parking lot beside it.',
  [W('General Schuyler House'),L('NPS: Schuyler House','https://www.nps.gov/places/schuyler-estate.htm'),NPSH,NPSD],th=[43.09512,-73.58248],thn='Schuyler House lot, US 4',brick='no'),
N('saratoga-monument-victory-woods','Saratoga Monument and Victory Woods','Victory','sar','landmark','standing',43.09853,-73.59327,'exact','public','walk','forts','1877–1887',
  'A 155-foot stone obelisk on the ground where Burgoyne\'s army entrenched in its last days before the surrender of October 17, 1777. The Saratoga Monument Association was formed in 1859; the cornerstone was laid on October 17, 1877, the capstone was set on November 3, 1882, and the bronze statues were finished in August 1887; it was handed to the State in 1912. Beside it, Victory Woods is the 22-acre site of the British army\'s final camp, where the men, women and children of the army spent a week under fire before surrendering.',
  'Bronze statues stand in three of the four niches: Schuyler facing east towards his estate, Daniel Morgan facing west and Gates facing north. The southern niche is empty, for Benedict Arnold. Victory Woods has a raised, accessible half-mile path with signs through the camp site.',
  'The empty niche.',
  'National Park Service. Grounds open sunrise to sunset; the monument\'s interior is closed for the 2026 season. Victory Woods is open daily in daylight; its path is not shovelled in winter. From US 4 turn onto Burgoyne Road (County Route 338): the monument is on the left after about three-quarters of a mile; Victory Woods parking is at the end of Monument Drive (NPS directions and hours pages, November 2026).',
  [W('Saratoga Battle Monument'),L('NPS: Saratoga Monument','https://www.nps.gov/places/saratoga-monument.htm'),L('NPS: Victory Woods','https://www.nps.gov/places/victory-woods.htm'),NPSH,NPSD],th=[43.09879,-73.59414],thn='Monument Drive loop by the Saratoga Monument (Victory Woods trail)',brick='no'),
N('old-saratoga-reformed-church','Old Saratoga Reformed Church','Schuylerville','sar','worship','standing',43.0984,-73.5845,'site','view','walk','','1857',
  'The congregation was organized in 1770 and its church was used as a hospital during the Revolutionary War. The present Greek Revival building at Burgoyne and Pearl Streets dates from 1857 and was listed on the Register in 1997.',
  'An 1857 Greek Revival church of red brick with a white columned portico and a white steeple (2010 photograph on Wikipedia), still a Reformed Church in America congregation. Interior not checked.',
  '',
  'A working church; see it from Burgoyne and Pearl Streets. Service times not checked.',
  [W('Old Saratoga Reformed Church')],brick='yes'),
N('st-stephens-schuylerville','St. Stephen\'s Episcopal Church','Schuylerville','sar','worship','standing',43.09667,-73.58389,'site','view','walk','','1868',
  'A cruciform Gothic Revival Episcopal church at 1 Grove Street, built in 1868 and listed on the Register in 2005.',
  'Quarry-faced stone laid in random ashlar, a steep gable roof and a polygonal steeple, both covered in patterned coloured slate. Interior not checked.',
  '',
  'Episcopal parish church; see it from Grove Street. Service times not checked.',
  [W('St. Stephen\'s Episcopal Church (Schuylerville, New York)')],brick='no'),
N('bullard-block-schuylerville','Bullard Block','Schuylerville','sar','civic','standing',43.09868,-73.58168,'site','view','walk','','1881',
  'A two-storey commercial block built in 1881 in the High Victorian Gothic style; it first housed the National Bank of Schuylerville. It was listed on the Register in 2009.',
  'Red brick (2009 photograph on Wikipedia) on a limestone foundation, with five storefront bays and pointed-arch upper windows, on Broad Street (US 4) in the village. Interior not checked.',
  '',
  'Private commercial building; see it from the street. Parking on the village streets not checked.',
  [W('Bullard Block (Schuylerville, New York)')],brick='yes'),
N('stillwater-united-church','Stillwater United Church (Second Baptist)','Stillwater','sar','worship','standing',42.93882,-73.65391,'exact','view','walk','','1873',
  'Built in 1873 as the Second Baptist Church to a design by Marcus F. Cummings of Troy, and now Stillwater United Church. A brick education wing was added in 1952. It was listed on the Register in 2006.',
  'A gable-roofed sanctuary in an early Romanesque or Northern Italianate manner over a raised basement of cut limestone, with an engaged brick bell tower rising to a belfry and a tall spire. Interior not checked.',
  '',
  'A working church on Hudson Avenue (Wikipedia gives number 135, OpenStreetMap 747); see it from the street. Service times not checked.',
  [W('Stillwater United Church')],conf='mixed',brick='some'),
N('copeland-covered-bridge','Copeland Covered Bridge','Edinburg','sar','landmark','standing',43.22093,-74.10031,'exact','public','drive','','1879',
  'Arad Copeland, who came from Guilford, Vermont, and ran a sawmill and carriage works on Beecher Creek, built this farm bridge in 1879 below Beecher Falls. A descendant gave it to the Nellie Tyrrell Edinburgh Museum and Edinburg Historical Society. It was listed on the Register in 1998.',
  'A small timber queenpost truss covered bridge with a gable roof, one span of about 30 to 35 feet (Wikipedia gives 30, the Covered Bridge Society 35) on fieldstone abutments over Beecher Creek. The Covered Bridge Society calls it the only covered bridge still standing in Saratoga County and one of two queenpost covered bridges in the state. It carries foot traffic only.',
  'The last covered bridge in Saratoga County.',
  'Kept by the Edinburg Historical Society and open to walkers. The Covered Bridge Society places it on County Route 4 off NY 30, Visit Sacandaga on North Shore Road; where to park not checked.',
  [W('Copeland Bridge'),L('New York State Covered Bridge Society: Copeland','https://www.nycoveredbridges.org/copeland/'),L('Visit Sacandaga: Copeland Bridge','https://visitsacandaga.com/?p=9908')],brick='no'),
]
